¶
{{smaller block|If God once willed adultery should be sinful, all his omnipotence will not allow him to will the allowance that his holiest people might, by his own ''antinomy'' or counterstatute, live unreproved.
{{right|''Milton'', Divorce, ii. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|Humility, poverty, meanness, and wretchedness are direct ''antinomies'' to the lusts of the flesh.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Great Exemplar, i. § 4.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
'''Antiochianism''' (an-ti-ō′ki-a̤n-izm), ''n.'' [< ''Antiochian'' + ''-ism.''] The name given to a school of theology which existed in the fourth and fifth centuries: so called because propagated chiefly by the church at Antioch, and also to distinguish it from Alexandrianism. {{smaller|It aimed at a middle course between the rigorously literal and the allegorical interpretation of the Scriptures.}}

'''antiodont''' (an′ti-ọ̄-dont), ''a.'' [< Gr. ἀντί, opposite to, + ὀδούς (ὀδοντ-) = E. ''tooth.''] Having a kind of lophodont dentition in which the folds or ridges of the molar crowns are opposite: opposed to ''amœbodont.''

'''antiopelmous''' (an″ti-ọ̄-pel′mus), ''a.'' [< Gr. ἀντίος, set against, + πέλμα, the sole.] In ''ornith.'', having an arrangement of the flexor tendons of the toes by which the flexor perforans supplies the third toe only, while the flexor hallucis splits into three tendons, passing to the first, second, and fourth toes.
{{smaller block|The synpelmous, the heteropelmous, and the ''antiopelmous'' arrangements are entirely peculiar to the present order [''Picariæ''].
{{right|''Stand. Nat. Hist.'', IV. 369.}}}}

'''anti-orgastic''' (an″ti-ôr-gas′tik), ''a.'' [< ''anti-'' + ''orgastic.''] Tending to allay excitement or venereal desire.

'''antipapal''' (an-ti-pā′pa̤l), ''a.'' [< ''anti-'' + ''papal.''] Opposed to the pope or to popery.
{{smaller block|He charges strictly his son after him to persevere in that ''antipapal'' schism.
{{right|''Milton'', Eikonoklastes, xxvii.}}}}

