¶
{{smaller block|The soil is . . . ''antipathetical to'' all venomous creatures.
{{right|''Howell'', Vocal Forest.}}}}

'''antipathic''' (an-ti-path′ik), ''a.'' [< NL. ''antipathicus'': see ''antipathy'' and ''-ic''.] '''1.''' Relating to antipathy; opposite; unlike; adverse.—'''2.''' Exciting antipathy. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Every one seems to have his ''antipathic'' animal.
{{right|''Kingsley'', Life, p. 41.}}}}

'''Antipathidæ''' (an-ti-path′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Antipathes'' + ''-idæ''.] A family of sclerobasic corals; the black corals, corresponding to the old genus ''Antipathes''. {{smaller|They have a branched fibrous axis and a soft friable cœnenchyma, which peels off after death, leaving the axial cœnosarc looking like a dry stick.}}

'''antipathise''', ''v.'' See ''antipathize''.

'''antipathist''' (an-tip′a̤-thist), ''n.'' [< ''antipathy'' + ''-ist''.] A person or thing having an antipathy to another, or being the direct opposite of another. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Sole positive of night!
''Antipathist'' of light.</poem>
{{right|''Coleridge'', Sibylline Leaves, II. 281.}}}}

'''antipathize''' (an-tip′a̤-thīz), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''antipathized'', ppr. ''antipathizing''. [< ''antipathy'' + ''-ize''.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' To feel antipathy or aversion; entertain or show a feeling, disposition, or opinion characterized by opposition or contrariety: the opposite of ''sympathize''. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|I must say I sympathise with Milverton and ''antipathise'' . . . with Lord Lytton.
{{right|''A. Helps'', Casimir Maremma, p. 39.}}}}
